    "result_text": "Full suite is green: **63 tests, 0 failures**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the household membership-level ceiling (Cp51 \"level-ceiling\") so a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum among their existing household members.\n\n**The tricky part** was that Cp51's test creates a *second* owner in the same household (same `lastName`+`postcode`) with only an **email** and *no* `sharesHousehold` flag, expecting success \u2014 while existing tests Cp10/Cp35 require exactly the same shape *without* an email to be rejected with 409, and Cp23/Cp39 build declared 3-member households whose last member legitimately reaches level 3 (which a naive cap would wrongly clip to 2). Reconciling all of these fixed the intended rule precisely.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.rejectDuplicateHousehold`** \u2014 a same-household create is now admitted (not 409) when it either declares `sharesHousehold` *or* supplies its own `email` (a distinguishing identifier marking a genuinely distinct member). No-email/no-flag creates still 409 (Cp10/Cp35 preserved).\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.assignMembershipLevelCap`** (new, called in `addOwner`) \u2014 for an *undeclared* joiner of an existing household, stores a cap of `max(existing household members' membershipLevel) + 1`. A household's first member and declared (`sharesHousehold`) members get no cap, so Cp23/Cp39's declared level-3 members are untouched. \"No existing household member \u2192 no cap.\"\n\n3. **`Owner`** \u2014 new persisted `membershipLevelCap` field; `getMembershipLevel()` returns the lesser of the points-derived level and the cap.\n\n4. **Schemas** (hsqldb/h2/mysql/postgres) \u2014 added the `membership_level_cap INTEGER` column (tests run on hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column is required in schema.sql).\n\nIn the Cp51 scenario the cap comes out to 2 and the second owner derives level 2, so it does not bind there, but the mechanism is fully in place per the specification.",
